From 968ab0e3480d93aee8698d7cbf727abf721f2a41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 16:43:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] rc: revert the 102.8 PREROUTING->VUPNP jump that DNATs all
 traffic

Reported by the upstream engineers while we were triaging the UPnP/console
failure, and confirmed here from the tree: 3006.102.8 carries a1ce74be78
("fix UPnP rules when port forwarding is not enabled", patch from Asus) whose
first hunk emits

    -A PREROUTING -j VUPNP

with no qualifier at all. VUPNP holds miniupnpd's DNAT redirects and is meant
to be entered only via "-A VSERVER -j VUPNP", where VSERVER is itself reached
as "-A PREROUTING -d <wan_ip> -j VSERVER" - i.e. only for packets addressed to
this router's WAN address. Unqualified, every packet crossing nat/PREROUTING is
tested against the UPnP redirects, OUTBOUND traffic included: a mapping on port
N rewrites a LAN client's own outgoing connection to a remote server on port N.
The field symptom is a console that cannot reach the game server at all while
UPnP is on. Asus resolved this internally and Merlin is reverting the patch for
102.8_4.

It was also unnecessary on this path. The VSERVER jump is emitted whether or not
port forwarding is enabled, and "-A VSERVER -j VUPNP" is gated on upnp_enable
rather than vts_enable_x, so UPnP redirects were already reachable - the problem
the patch set out to fix does not exist here.

Reverting the first hunk ONLY. The patch's other hunk ("-A FORWARD -j FUPNP")
is KEPT deliberately: it is the sole hook into FUPNP, it carries filter-table
ACCEPTs, and it cannot DNAT anything - dropping it would leave UPnP forwards
translated and then dropped in FORWARD. The `char tmp[32]` the patch added to
nat_setting() goes with the reverted hunk, since nothing else in that function
used it and it would now warn.

This is a DIFFERENT bug from the IGD:1/IGD:2 description issue fixed in
ac485cfd4a - the engineers were explicit that the two are unrelated, and also
confirmed the IGD:2 diagnosis independently: "IGDv2 compatibility is indeed
broken in a lot of clients, including Windows itself... People should stick
with V1 unless they need IPv6 pinhole support." That is exactly the shape
already shipped here (V1 default, pinholes force V2).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 release/src/router/rc/firewall.c | 36 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/release/src/router/rc/firewall.c b/release/src/router/rc/firewall.c
index 54ff5bef8b..0b0d3bd9a8 100644
--- a/release/src/router/rc/firewall.c
+++ b/release/src/router/rc/firewall.c
@@ -1689,7 +1689,9 @@ void nat_setting(char *wan_if, char *wan_ip, char *wanx_if, char *wanx_ip, char
 	FILE *fp;
 	char lan_class[32];
 	char name[PATH_MAX];
-	char tmp[32];
+	/* char tmp[32]; - came in with a1ce74be78 for the PREROUTING->VUPNP jump
+	 * reverted below; nothing else in this function used it, so it would now be
+	 * an unused-variable warning. */
 	int wan_unit;
 #ifdef RTCONFIG_MULTIWAN_CFG
 	int wanx_rules = 0;
@@ -1800,10 +1802,34 @@ void nat_setting(char *wan_if, char *wan_ip, char *wanx_if, char *wanx_ip, char
 #endif
 #endif
 
-	/* Add rule to jump to VUPNP chain when UPNP is enabled*/
-	snprintf(tmp, sizeof(tmp), "wan%d_upnp_enable", wan_primary_ifunit());
-	if (nvram_get_int(tmp) == 1)
-		fprintf(fp, "-A PREROUTING -j VUPNP\n");
+	/* reaper 2026-08-13: REVERTED - this is the 3006.102.8 "all traffic gets
+	 * DNAT" bug. Upstream (Asus, then Merlin) reverted the same patch; it is
+	 * due back out in 102.8_4. The stock line here was:
+	 *
+	 *     snprintf(tmp, sizeof(tmp), "wan%d_upnp_enable", wan_primary_ifunit());
+	 *     if (nvram_get_int(tmp) == 1)
+	 *             fprintf(fp, "-A PREROUTING -j VUPNP\n");
+	 *
+	 * added by a1ce74be78 ("fix UPnP rules when port forwarding is not
+	 * enabled", patch from Asus). VUPNP holds miniupnpd's DNAT rules and is
+	 * meant to be reached ONLY via "-A VSERVER -j VUPNP", where VSERVER is
+	 * itself entered as "-A PREROUTING -d <wan_ip> -j VSERVER" - i.e. only for
+	 * packets actually addressed to this router's WAN address. This jump had NO
+	 * destination qualifier, so every packet crossing nat/PREROUTING was tested
+	 * against the UPnP redirects, OUTBOUND traffic included: a mapping on port
+	 * N would rewrite a LAN client's own outgoing connection to a remote server
+	 * on port N. Field symptom was a console that could not reach the game
+	 * server at all while UPnP was on.
+	 *
+	 * It was also redundant. The VSERVER jump is emitted whether or not port
+	 * forwarding is enabled, and "-A VSERVER -j VUPNP" is gated on upnp_enable
+	 * rather than on vts_enable_x, so UPnP redirects were already reachable -
+	 * the problem the patch set out to solve did not exist on this path.
+	 *
+	 * The patch's OTHER hunk ("-A FORWARD -j FUPNP", further down) is KEPT: it
+	 * is the only hook into FUPNP, it carries filter-table ACCEPTs, and it
+	 * cannot DNAT anything. Dropping it would leave UPnP forwards translated
+	 * and then dropped in FORWARD. */
 
 	/* VSERVER chain */
 	if (inet_addr_(wan_ip)) {
